Consider the equation $z^6+z^3+1=0$. Since the equation leads to a sextic polynomial, it has at most 6 roots. To find complex roots when the real coefficients are given, one common strategy is to look for a complex root with a magnitude of 1, i.e., a root on the unit circle. Looking for roots in polar coordinates can simplify the process. We rewrite our equation as

\begin{equation}
z^6+z^3+1 = z^6 + (1+e^{3i\theta})z^3 + 1=0
\end{equation}

Now we express the complex cube root of unity in terms of polar coordinates,

\begin{equation}
e^{3i\theta} = e^{i(3\theta + 2\pi n)}, \quad n \in \mathbb{Z}.
\end{equation}

Substituting $z=e^{i\phi}$ gives

\begin{align}
e^{6i\phi}+(1+e^{3i\theta})e^{3i\phi}+1 &= e^{6i\phi}+(1 + e^{i(3\theta + 2\pi n)})e^{3i\phi}+1\\
&= e^{6i\phi} + e^{3i\phi}e^{i(3\theta + 2\pi n)} + e^{3i\phi} + 1\\
&= e^{3i\phi}(e^{3i\phi} + e^{i(3\theta + 2\pi n)} + 1) = 0.
\end{align}

Since the real part at the left of this equation is non-negative, we require the real part of the factor $e^{3i\phi} + e^{i(3\theta + 2\pi n)} + 1$ to be zero:

\begin{equation}
e^{3\phi} + \cos{3\theta} + \cos(3\phi + 3\theta + 2\pi n) = 0.
\end{equation}

Let $0 \leqslant \theta < 2\pi$ to have a single solution for $\theta$. Simplifying, we have

\begin{equation}
\cos{3\theta} = -\frac{1}{2}\left(e^{3\phi} + e^{-3\phi}+1\right).
\end{equation}

Now we look for an initial value to our variables, for example, let $\phi=\frac{\pi}{6}$. By plugging $\phi$ into the Equation 5, we can find

\begin{equation}
\cos{3\theta} = -\frac{1}{2}\left(-\sqrt{3}+1+1\right) = 2\sqrt{3} \implies 3\theta \approx 1.273\pi \implies \theta \approx 60.19^\circ
\end{equation}

We have only looked at one possible root and found that it is close to $60^\circ$. Since $3\theta$ is close to $2\pi$ and $6\theta$ is evenly spaced on the circle, we can deduce that the roots lie approximately at arguments of $30^\circ$, $90^\circ$, $150^\circ$, $210^\circ$, $270^\circ$ and $330^\circ$.   Since we want a root between $90^\circ$ and $180^\circ$, we can confirm $\theta = 135^\circ$ solves our problem.

\boxed{135^\circ}

